{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nThe MCP endpoint became unreachable on 2026-08-28 (other sessions reported \"rebar's MCP is\ndown\"). The container was healthy — a local probe to `127.0.0.1:8092/health` returned `200` in\n1.8ms — but external requests to `https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp` timed out. Five\nprobes sent ~12s apart were all logged by the server within the SAME millisecond, i.e. queued\nand flushed in a burst. nginx was buffering to `/var/lib/nginx/tmp/` on a root disk at **92%\n(2.6G free)**.\n\nReclaiming docker garbage took the disk to **57% (13G free)** and the endpoint recovered\nimmediately: six consecutive external probes returned `401` in ~250ms.\n\n## Why the automatic reclaim did not self-heal it\n\n`prune_docker_caches()` (`infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh:276-284`) runs exactly two commands, and\nneither could reclaim what had accumulated:\n\n    timeout 120 docker builder prune -f --keep-storage \"$BUILD_CACHE_KEEP\"   # BUILD_CACHE_KEEP=5GB\n    timeout 120 docker image prune -f                                        # no -a\n\n1. **The build cache was capped at exactly what was filling the disk.** `BUILD_CACHE_KEEP`\n   defaults to `5GB` (`:124`) and the cache measured **5.254GB**. The prune was working as\n   designed and retaining it.\n2. **`docker image prune -f` without `-a` removes only DANGLING images.** The comment at `:272`\n   states the intent: *\"TAGGED images are never touched (:prev is the rollback lifeline)\"*. But\n   blue-green tags every build with an immutable per-release tag, so the box held **44 images /\n   11.09GB, of which 10.08GB was reclaimable**. Tagged-image accumulation is the dominant leak\n   and nothing is permitted to remove it.\n\n### The actual tag scheme (corrected 2026-08-28)\n\nAn earlier revision of this ticket called the per-release image `rebar-mcp-<sha>`. That is\nwrong, and the correction is load-bearing for the fix:\n\n* **Image tag** — `\"$MCP_IMAGE:$TARGET\"`, i.e. `compose-mcp:<40-char-sha>`, produced by the\n  `docker tag` at `autodeploy.sh:763` and consumed by `mcp_run_container` at `:406`.\n  `MCP_IMAGE` defaults to `compose-mcp` (`:146`), so ALL releases share the single image\n  NAME `compose-mcp` and differ only in the TAG.\n* **Container name** — `\"${MCP_CONTAINER_PREFIX}-${TARGET:0:12}-${mcp_newport}\"` (`:783`),\n  i.e. `rebar-mcp-<sha12>-<port>`. `MCP_CONTAINER_PREFIX` defaults to `rebar-mcp` (`:147`).\n  So `rebar-mcp-<sha>` names a CONTAINER, never an image.\n\nBecause the container name carries only a TRUNCATED sha and the port, the container name must\nNOT be string-manipulated back into an image tag. The per-container SHA maps to its image tag\nby ASKING DOCKER: `docker inspect -f '{{.Config.Image}}' <container>` returns the exact\n`compose-mcp:<sha>` reference that container was started from. That is the identifier the\nretire path removes.\n\nSo the gate ran on schedule, logged `\"disk pressure reclaim complete\"`, freed ~nothing, then\nthrottled for `PRESSURE_PRUNE_MIN_INTERVAL` (600s) and repeated that misleading success.\n\n`infra/scripts/observability.sh:330-335` says the marker exists so an incident sweep can\n*\"distinguish 'the reclaim gate never ran' from 'it ran and reclaimed nothing'\"* without host\naccess. It cannot: it counts INVOCATIONS, not bytes freed, so both cases look identical.\n\n## Alerting was not the gap — recovery was\n\n`rebar-root-disk-pressure` (threshold 85%, SNS `rebar-gerrit-alerts`) **did fire**: ALARM at\n2026-08-27T23:48 PDT, cleared 2026-08-28T10:45 PDT only when the manual reclaim landed —\n**~11 hours in ALARM**. It flapped beforehand (22:29 / 22:51 / 23:09 / 23:48) as the ineffective\nreclaim freed a little and the disk refilled. A threshold alarm cannot say \"reclaim is running\nand achieving nothing\", which is the actionable condition.\n\n## Scope (operator-directed, 2026-08-28)\n\n1. **Retire the per-release `compose-mcp:<sha>` image once its container is fully drained and\n   reaped.** The blue-green retire path (`mcp_retire_sweep`) already reaps exited containers\n   with `docker rm`; removing that container's image belongs there — AFTER the reap succeeds,\n   never while serving. The image reference is read with\n   `docker inspect -f '{{.Config.Image}}' <container>` BEFORE the `docker rm` (it is\n   unreadable afterwards). The rollback lifeline is retained explicitly: the bare\n   `$MCP_IMAGE` / `$MCP_IMAGE:latest` build tag, `$MCP_IMAGE:prev`, and the image of the LIVE\n   backend are never removed. Removal uses `docker image rm` WITHOUT `-f`, so the daemon\n   itself refuses while any container still references the image — a second, independent guard\n   against removing a serving release.\n2. **Log root-disk free space before and after every reclaim**, plus the delta, so effect is\n   observable rather than inferred.\n3. **Emit a loud, distinct marker when pressure persists across 3 or more reclaim cycles** —\n   the \"reclaim is ineffective\" signal that no existing alarm expresses.\n\n### Persistence and configurability of the streak counter\n\nThe reclaim gate runs as a SEPARATE `autodeploy.sh` process on every ~2-min timer tick, so the\nconsecutive-ineffective-cycle counter must be persisted on disk between invocations. It mirrors\nthe existing `PRESSURE_PRUNE_TS_FILE` pattern exactly (`autodeploy.sh:133`): a file under\n`$STATE_DIR` named by an overridable `PRESSURE_STREAK_FILE`, written via write-tmp-then-`mv`\n(atomic rename, so a torn write is never observed). It **fails safe toward silence**: an\nabsent, unreadable, or non-numeric file reads as `0`, so a lost or corrupt counter delays the\npersistence marker rather than emitting a false one. The alarm threshold is a named,\noverridable constant `PRESSURE_STREAK_ALARM` (default `3`), consistent with the existing\n`DISK_PRESSURE_PCT` / `PRESSURE_PRUNE_MIN_INTERVAL` tunables — not a hardcoded literal.\n\n### Out of scope: the pre-existing tag backlog\n\nThe reclaim is retire-path-only, so it removes an image only when a container is reaped AFTER\nthis change ships. Tags orphaned earlier — including any reaped inside the crash window between\n`docker rm` and the image removal — have no future reap event and are accepted as residue.\nThat is sufficient: the incident backlog was already cleared manually (44 images -> 4), so the\nsteady state after this change is bounded, and a periodic unconditional `docker image prune -a`\nis explicitly NOT adopted because it cannot distinguish the live release and `:prev` from\ngarbage. Draining any residue remains a manual operator action.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] After a blue-green container is reaped, the `compose-mcp:<sha>` image it was running is\n      removed, while the live backend's image and `$MCP_IMAGE:prev` are retained (checked by a\n      test that reaps a fake container and asserts exactly which image references survive).\n- [ ] A serving container's image is NEVER removed — the retire path removes an image only\n      after the `docker rm` for that container has succeeded (checked by a test that seeds a\n      RUNNING container and asserts no image removal is issued for it).\n- [ ] `prune_docker_caches` logs root-disk free space BEFORE and AFTER, and the delta, on every\n      invocation (checked by asserting both values and the delta appear in the emitted log line).\n- [ ] When a reclaim completes and the disk is STILL at or above `DISK_PRESSURE_PCT`, the\n      persisted streak counter increments; it resets to zero once the disk falls below the\n      threshold (checked by driving the counter across a pressure/relief sequence).\n- [ ] At `PRESSURE_STREAK_ALARM` (default 3) or more consecutive ineffective cycles, a distinct\n      marker is emitted (not the existing `AUTODEPLOY_DISK_PRESSURE`, which counts invocations)\n      and `observability.sh` publishes it as its own delta metric, so it is alarmable without\n      host access.\n- [ ] The new marker fires only on PERSISTENCE: a single pressured cycle that then recovers does\n      NOT emit it (checked by a negative-control test — this is the discriminator, since an\n      alarm that fires on any pressure is the flapping alarm we already have).\n\n## Evidence\n\nDisk 92%->57%; images 44->4 (11.09GB->2.779GB); build cache 5.254GB->0. Endpoint went from four\n12s timeouts to six 401s at ~250ms. Volumes were deliberately untouched (11 present, 1 active)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787942238746642001,"uuid":"b4072b3a-2085-498c-8464-338619711023"}